Let the power-of-two multiply fast path be switched off, for C-20.

ecma_integer_multiply turns a multiply by a power of two into a shift, and gets the
shift distance from __builtin_ctz. That builtin crashes the Capstone backend:

  LegalizeDAG.cpp:1352: "Type mismatch for custom legalized operation"
  failing node: t11: i32 = cttz_zero_undef t10

Minimal reproducer and the full account, including a hypothesis that looked right
and was refuted by test, are in tests/compiler-repros/C20-cttz-i32-crashes-legalizer/.
__builtin_clz and __builtin_popcount are unaffected; -mattr=+zbb avoids it but no
build in this tree passes +zbb and whether the silicon implements it is not
established.

THIS IS A WORKAROUND FOR A COMPILER DEFECT, NOT A PORTABILITY FIX, and it is written
so it can be deleted when C-20 is: the block is guarded by JERRY_NO_BUILTIN_CTZ
rather than removed, so reversing this costs one -D.

Why it is safe to switch off. The block is a pure optimisation. Three lines below it
the function ends with the general form

    return ecma_make_integer_value (left_integer * right_integer);

which is what runs with the fast path disabled. Semantics are unchanged; a multiply
by a power of two is computed as a multiply instead of a shift.

Noted in passing and NOT relied on: the guarded path looks wrong upstream anyway.
`(left & (left - 1)) == 0` is also true for left == 0, where __builtin_ctz(0) is
undefined, and for INT32_MIN, where `right << 31` is not `right * INT32_MIN`. That is
an upstream question, not ours, and this patch neither fixes nor depends on it.

--- a/jerry-core/ecma/base/ecma-helpers-number.c	2026-08-19 12:46:37.862865431 +0800
+++ b/jerry-core/ecma/base/ecma-helpers-number.c	2026-08-19 12:46:37.890865430 +0800
@@ -409,7 +409,7 @@
 ecma_integer_multiply (ecma_integer_value_t left_integer, /**< left operand */
                        ecma_integer_value_t right_integer) /**< right operand */
 {
-#if defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__)
+#if (defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__)) && !defined(JERRY_NO_BUILTIN_CTZ)
   /* Check if either integer is power of 2 */
   if (JERRY_UNLIKELY ((left_integer & (left_integer - 1)) == 0))
   {
